00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.280
So clicking down a level into
high-performance computing,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.870
I'd like to talk about
what HPC is first.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.961
Sometimes you talk to people
and they're new to HPC.

00:03:00.961 --> 00:03:02.960
Sometimes they have a
very different definition.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.510
At Intel, we think of HPC
as an activity that really

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.110
has three characteristics.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.830
The first one is the purpose
of the calculation is actually

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:10.760
to gain some insight.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.135
You're trying to
learn something.

00:03:12.135 --> 00:03:13.516
There's an intellectual product.

00:03:13.516 --> 00:03:16.140
The second characteristic is it
has to be a demanding workload.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.598
It's not low-performance
computing.

00:03:17.598 --> 00:03:19.057
This is high-performance
computing.

00:03:19.057 --> 00:03:20.514
And the third
characteristic, which

00:03:20.514 --> 00:03:22.970
I think is really important,
is when you appropriately

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.350
apply more resources
to the problem,

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.120
you actually get a
more valuable result.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.360
So think of one of these
examples like weather

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.480
forecasting.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.490
You can do a weather
forecast on your phone,

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.060
but you get a 12-hour
forecast and maybe 30 days.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.570
It has zero value.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.854
But if you can run that
thing in three hours

00:03:39.854 --> 00:03:41.270
and now determine
that something's

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:42.890
going to happen you need
to evacuate some people

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.265
or change your
plans, it can have

00:03:44.265 --> 00:03:47.830
tremendous value, financial
value and just personal value.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.580
And those examples,
there are many of them

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:53.447
across many fields that do
high-performance computing.

00:03:53.447 --> 00:03:56.030
And it may be surprising to you
but we think of data analytics

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:57.500
and machine learning, a
lot of that, actually,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.208
is a high-performance
computing workload.

00:03:59.208 --> 00:04:03.110
And if you apply those
three tests, you'll see why.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.869
So why are people
thinking about the cloud

00:04:05.869 --> 00:04:07.160
for high-performance computing?

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.204
Some people think it's
a bit of an oxymoron.

00:04:09.204 --> 00:04:11.620
High-performance computing is
about supercomputers, right?

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.000
Well, if you go to any
supercomputing site, what

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.060
you'll find is they have
some very large applications

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.913
and workloads that are
driving the characteristics

00:04:19.913 --> 00:04:21.079
and the size of the machine.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:25.430
But they have many, many smaller
workloads that could actually

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:26.640
run somewhere else.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.040
So it's still HPC.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.400
Not every workload needs
that machine, necessarily.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.160
Those machines, though, tend
to be completely full, 100%

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.120
utilization.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.530
And there's always a backlog.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.149
And as soon as the
queue gets shorter,

00:04:38.149 --> 00:04:40.190
engineers, scientists,
whoever's using that thing

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:41.690
are putting more jobs in.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.640
So there's always
this pent-up demand.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:45.740
And so one of the main reasons
people are looking to the cloud

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.156
is because some
of their workloads

00:04:47.156 --> 00:04:48.620
can run well in
the cloud and they

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.810
can manage those demand surges.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.570
And by taking some of the
workloads that maybe aren't

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.070
demanding the
on-premise resources

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:56.360
and putting them in the
cloud, they actually

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.400
make their on-premise
system even more valuable

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.140
while it stays full.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.150
There's also getting
access to new technologies,

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:05.520
like I mentioned, earliest
access to Skylake in the cloud.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.405
And also R&amp;D collaboration,
think of teams

00:05:07.405 --> 00:05:08.280
all around the world.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.988
Rather than shipping
data back and forth,

00:05:09.988 --> 00:05:12.000
we take advantage of
the tremendous network

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.500
that Google Cloud
has and it enables

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.500
people to collaborate
much more easily than they

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.572
have been able to in the past.

00:05:19.572 --> 00:05:21.030
So how do you decide
what workloads

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.462
should fit in the Cloud?

00:05:22.462 --> 00:05:23.920
There's really two
characteristics.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.190
The first one is, do
you trust the Cloud?

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.080
And I would argue
that in the past

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.169
people have felt that the Cloud
maybe wasn't as secure and as

00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:32.460
safe as keeping things at home.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.560
I think those
perceptions are changing.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.300
Some of the best security in
the world is in the Cloud.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.280
So there's this
boundary of public Cloud

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.190
that's rising as
a result of that.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.860
In terms of technical
characteristics, as I said,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.530
there's a wide range of
workloads that run in HPC.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.480
And any of them could
run on a dedicated HPC

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.110
system or a supercomputer.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.110
But many of them
over on the left

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:55.050
where they don't necessarily
need to scale as far,

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.730
they don't need
as tight coupling

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.250
for message passing, which
is the predominant way

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.800
that high-performance
computing applications run,

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:02.890
these are a great fit.

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:05.847
And so you see in industries
like finance and genomics,

00:06:05.847 --> 00:06:06.930
that's where things start.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.360
Or in manufacturing, I
showed them on the boundary

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.520
where some of the
early calculations

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.650
to do design studies
are done in the Cloud.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.170
And final studies for
noise vibration harshness,

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:19.430
combustion, car crash
simulation, and safety

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.860
are run, still, on a
traditional HPC system.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:24.750
So finding the right
workloads is key,

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.780
and putting those in the Cloud.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.480
So behind HPC in the Cloud
and behind the Google Cloud

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.550
we have the Intel Xeon
scalable processor family.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.090
This is our latest
generation, codenamed Skylake.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.010
And we drove a
lot of innovations

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.800
into this processor
specifically to meet

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.080
the needs of high-performance
computing workloads.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.060
They have very broad
benefits, but HPC

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.740
was one of the main drivers.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.406
An example is moving from the
previous generation's Ring

00:06:50.406 --> 00:06:51.780
architecture to
connect the cores

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.480
to a dual two-dimensional mesh.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.460
There's also tremendous
cache enhancements,

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.460
the addition of
AVX-512 instructions,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.370
that allows us to do twice as
many FLOPS per clock tick--

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.500
Floating Point Operations.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.070
We increase the memory
bandwidth by a factor of two

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.110
over the previous generation.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:12.300
And finally, we increased
the number of cores

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.930
and the efficiency
of hyperthreading.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.810
And as a result, people
are able to run much more

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.430
dense calculations
on a single CPU.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:26.850
So you're seeing many more
virtual machines, virtual CPUs,

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:30.890
inside of a VM now are possible.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:32.360
So where are these available?

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:35.480
Google's been tremendously
aggressive in getting these

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:36.350
out.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.600
And so I think this slide
is already out of date

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.090
since we made it
just a few weeks ago.

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:42.470
There were 13 regions.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.670
Google is adding Skylake
instances all around the world.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:48.230
So if you want to use
these, all you have to do

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:50.810
is say the minimum CPU
and specify this family.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:53.480
In fact, Bill will talk about
that a bit in his section.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:55.790
And you can get access
to these and try it out

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:57.950
and see how well they
do for your own workload

00:07:57.950 --> 00:08:00.380
because at the end of the
day, that's what matters.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:02.450
But I do want to show you
some examples from some

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.090
of our customers and
what they're saying.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:07.370
I think Decartes Lab is
a really interesting,

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:08.780
interesting company.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:12.890
They are mining 1 petabyte of
satellite imagery from NASA

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:15.710
from the last 40 years
to look at what's

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:18.140
going on and out of
the satellite data,

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:22.100
correlate it to the history of
the food supply in the world

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.620
and using it to
predict crop yields.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.830
This impacts the financial
services markets.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.820
It impacts food security
around the world.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.130
This is an application of
high-performance computing,

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.010
machine learning, and
obviously very, very big data.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.460
And they've just, in this
move to the latest generation,

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.309
saw a 40% uptick almost
in their performance.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:41.809
And you can see
other examples where

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.990
people are seeing
almost 2x, especially

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:48.970
in the life sciences area, the
biotech companies at the right.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:51.111
So Descartes actually
has a session tomorrow.

00:08:51.111 --> 00:08:53.110
If you want to hear more
about their experience,

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:56.280
you can go look at it.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.700
So a lot of this
performance does come to you

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.340
quite naturally if you've
modernized your code.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.290
But I do want to put
one caveat out there

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.979
and say, if you haven't
modernized your code,

00:09:06.979 --> 00:09:08.520
you're just counting
on the processor

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.450
to make things faster,
you may be missing out.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:13.410
So this is an example
from financial services

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.650
of a binomial tree
options calculation.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:20.280
And you can see there's a 130x
performance difference over

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.480
the seven years that comes from
someone who actively optimized

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.430
the code versus
someone who didn't.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:28.080
So you need to thread your code.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.554
You need to vectorize your code.

00:09:29.554 --> 00:09:31.470
You need to work with
the Intel compilers that

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:34.710
unleash the performance
of this processor.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.770
Otherwise, you could be leaving
100x performance on the table.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.410
This is a synthetic
benchmark, but we see it

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.640
in real applications, as well.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:43.920
So it is work.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.870
But we have things to
help you out with that.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.840
So what does Intel
do to help you?

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.950
We're talking about
modernizing your code.

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:51.850
Well, the first
thing we need to do

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:53.370
is provide a
development methodology

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.560
for your high-performance
computing applications

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.018
that allows you to
understand where

00:09:57.018 --> 00:09:58.920
you're leaving
performance on the table

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.690
and then go chase it down.

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.700
So some things we
recommend, first one, out

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.530
of this Parallel Studio XE
is use optimized libraries.

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:08.317
These have been hand-tuned
for the latest processors that

00:10:08.317 --> 00:10:09.150
are out there today.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.390
They're launched
with the processors.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:11.610
So if you're using
math libraries,

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:12.990
you're getting the performance.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.420
Many, many applications
in HPC do that.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.220
The second one is compile.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.990
Our compilers are
updated well ahead

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:21.030
of the release of a
processor and allow

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:24.330
you to actually update
your application.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.170
It'll still run on
previous generations.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.045
But will be optimized
for the new generation,

00:10:28.045 --> 00:10:30.090
be ready to go
when it comes out.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.040
We have analysis
tools, again, that

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:34.470
point you to not only where
you're leaving performance

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.260
on the table, but
also tell you how

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.810
you can extract that performance
by making recommendations

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.655
around vectorization,
recommendations

00:10:41.655 --> 00:10:43.530
around threading, and
even projecting for you

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:47.200
the type of performance that
you could get our advisor tool.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:49.080
And finally, our
VTune tool shows

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:52.020
you the results of your
analysis and whether or not

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:53.920
you got the performance
you expected.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.140
And if not, why not?

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.350
By using the hardware counters
that exist in our processors.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:01.542
And there's some very
advanced analysis techniques.

00:11:01.542 --> 00:11:03.000
And finally,
performance is nothing

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:04.530
if your application
isn't correct.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.279
And anyone who's done
parallel programming

00:11:06.279 --> 00:11:09.516
knows that multi-threading,
vectorization,

00:11:09.516 --> 00:11:11.890
if you do them incorrectly,
you can get the wrong result.

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:13.800
So we have a whole
suite of tools

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.740
that analyze and will help you
find those types of problems

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.200
automatically, which are
tremendous productivity

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:19.950
boosters.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:22.860
So this is exactly what we
recommend for on-prem systems.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.630
It's exactly what you
need to do in the Cloud.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.710
So we've put a lot of
effort in with Google

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:30.210
to make sure that these tools
run and run well in the Cloud.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.689
So please check out
the Parallel Studio XE

00:11:32.689 --> 00:11:34.980
if you're going to be running
applications in the Cloud

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.410
that you write yourself.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.080
So last thing I want to say
before I hand it over to Bill

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:41.994
is, I've talked about CPUs.

00:11:41.994 --> 00:11:43.410
I've talked about
the motivations.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.990
But there's a big
distance between coming up

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.180
from the bottom and
having the infrastructure

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:50.400
interfaces that you get
through Google Cloud.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:53.340
And what HPC users
and HPC applications

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:56.190
expect coming from the top
and the link between those two

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:57.870
worlds to make it
possible for you

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.660
to continue using
your on-prem resources

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:02.040
but to put those same
applications, perhaps

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:05.289
with different input sets,
in the Cloud is consistency.

00:12:05.289 --> 00:12:07.080
So Intel has been
working with the industry

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:11.610
for over a decade to document a
standard cluster architecture.

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:13.500
And it's been
adopted very broadly.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.600
Altair's been one of our
great partners in this.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.272
And their appliances
actually implement

00:12:18.272 --> 00:12:19.980
this and as a result
are very compatible,

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:21.521
not only with their
own applications,

00:12:21.521 --> 00:12:23.620
but from applications
across the industry.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:26.284
So with that, I'd like to
introduce Bill Nitzberg, who

00:12:26.284 --> 00:12:28.450
will take you through some
of those usability things

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:29.920
and making HPC
work in the Cloud.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:30.461
Thanks, Bill.

