00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:12.220
Gives you real time
monitoring, reporting.

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:16.830
Really nice feature that we've
added is a workload simulator.

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:19.564
And I don't have a demo of
this, so I'll just say it.

00:15:19.564 --> 00:15:21.730
So one of the problems in
high-performance computing

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:23.396
is these are really
complicated systems.

00:15:23.396 --> 00:15:25.510
Even if you're using--
even if you're all cloud,

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:28.120
it's a complicated
infrastructure

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:29.482
with a complicated workload.

00:15:29.482 --> 00:15:31.690
And if you want to optimize
exactly what you're using

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:34.310
and how you're using it,
it's nice to be able to say,

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:36.850
hey, here's what I
did last quarter.

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:39.660
Could you tell me if I
changed a few things around,

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:43.347
if I used larger memory nodes,
how fast things would things

00:15:43.347 --> 00:15:43.930
have got done?

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:45.430
Would we have had
faster turnaround?

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.280
So we have a simulator built
in that lets you ask and answer

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:49.390
that question.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:51.460
And then cloudbursting.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:54.430
On the Access side,
we really have

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:57.520
been trying to re-imagine
what engineers do.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:59.000
So I'm a geek.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:15:59.830
I'm a nerd.

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:01.301
And so I live in command line.

00:16:01.301 --> 00:16:03.550
I'm happy to install Linux,
although I haven't done it

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:04.210
in many years.

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:06.940
But I'm happy to, really.

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:11.320
And what we found, though, is
that's great at the very, very

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:12.327
high end.

00:16:12.327 --> 00:16:13.910
People really know
what they're doing.

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:14.614
They're experts.

00:16:14.614 --> 00:16:16.780
But if you want to take
high-performance computing--

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.363
which, by the way, my definition
of high-performance computing

00:16:19.363 --> 00:16:22.240
is taking computing up to the
absolute maximum performance

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:23.800
level you can.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.770
Not quite just to where
it almost doesn't work,

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:27.850
but right up to the edge.

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.391
So performance is paramount.

00:16:29.391 --> 00:16:31.890
And so at that level, you have
a lot of really smart people.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.470
They don't mind
doing a lot of stuff.

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:35.860
But if you want to make that
available to other people

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:40.870
down lower, what we've found
is that the engineers of today

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:43.570
are not as happy doing all
the nitty gritty stuff.

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:46.360
And in fact, if you
can give an interface--

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:47.390
this is crazy.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:49.600
I don't believe it, but
I actually believe it.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:51.010
That sounds crazy.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:55.390
If you could give an interface
that requires zero Linux shell

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:59.040
or zero little bits about IT,
you can actually attract, like,

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:02.980
100 times more people in
terms of just learning curve.

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:06.388
So if you can get nobody
to have to type ls or cp,

00:17:06.388 --> 00:17:08.930
you actually get this
huge extra set of people.

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:10.210
And so that's our vision.

