00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:49.830
At Altair, like I said,
we have this broad range

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:50.497
of applications.

00:19:50.497 --> 00:19:51.954
And one of the
things that you want

00:19:51.954 --> 00:19:53.550
to do in high-performance
computing

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.590
is you want to perform
as fast as possible.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.733
So these are physics
applications that do the work.

00:19:59.733 --> 00:20:02.370
As in the end, one
of the nice things

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:04.650
about our partnership
with Intel,

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:06.750
we get access to
Intel processors.

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.360
And we can actually do
sorts of neat tests.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:13.770
And we did one of those tests
with RADIOSS, a crash code,

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:16.990
and found that, actually, it
runs 1.8 times faster on Intel

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:18.211
Skylake.

00:20:18.211 --> 00:20:20.460
There's two examples I'll
give of how you can actually

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:23.310
choose Intel Skylake because if
you want to get performance--

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.643
if you're doing high performance
computing, you want to.

00:20:25.643 --> 00:20:30.960
So one is when you're creating
an instance on Google Cloud,

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:32.680
you can just pick
[? i1 ?] Skylake.

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:34.830
And for that, you're going
to actually get Skylake.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:36.390
You'll get this
1.8 times faster.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:41.790
But how do you actually get--

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:44.520
most high performance
computing today

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:48.900
is actually done in private
systems on supercomputers.

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:53.910
And even though it's
the most leading edge

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.394
newest work that
people are working on,

00:20:56.394 --> 00:20:58.560
the organizations are a
little slow to adopt things.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.690
So most of the work actually
still happens in-house

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:02.130
on private clusters.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.631
And we're just
connecting the Cloud.

00:21:03.631 --> 00:21:05.546
And the way that we're
connecting to the Cloud

00:21:05.546 --> 00:21:06.690
is through cloud bursting.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.606
If you haven't heard it
before, all that means

00:21:08.606 --> 00:21:09.930
is building a hybrid system.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:11.700
A lot of work's
happening locally.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:13.620
When you have specific
work that fits,

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:15.930
you actually create an
instance on the cloud

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:17.980
to run that local work.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.640
And with the PBS works tools,
we make that really seamless.

