00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:20.060
in terms of Appsbroker
experts that

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:22.370
have been working with
Google for 12 years

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:23.740
on implementing Google Platform.

00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:28.340
So we understand Google, but
leveraging the best of Intel

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:30.000
and Google resources, as well.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:31.920
Now, why is that important?

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:34.730
Because one of the reasons
why a lot of the change

00:28:34.730 --> 00:28:38.660
isn't happening is because
it's about creating

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:39.800
business awareness.

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:42.180
Business leaders don't
know what's possible.

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:46.427
It's also around creating
technical awareness,

00:28:46.427 --> 00:28:49.010
helping people understand what
performance is really possible,

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:51.770
people that typically
have their own platforms.

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:55.220
And also it's very much around
creating a change in culture,

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:57.410
as well, one where
people are thinking

00:28:57.410 --> 00:29:00.290
about using new features all
the time and not standalone,

00:29:00.290 --> 00:29:02.600
static systems.

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:06.650
So we talked earlier
about extreme cloud.

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:10.370
Bill mentioned the use cases
in a number of industries.

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:13.070
And yes, right now, using
the Google Cloud Platform

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:14.810
for big companies
means you can do

00:29:14.810 --> 00:29:18.320
far more processing, far more
analysis, far more simulations.

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:22.579
You can get really good
insights at very low cost.

00:29:22.579 --> 00:29:24.870
The challenge you were hearing
from a lot of customers,

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:28.429
though, is, well, if we
start adopting the Cloud,

00:29:28.429 --> 00:29:30.470
how are we going to have
a roadmap of innovation?

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:32.750
And that's something that Google
is answering with the Intel

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:33.470
relationship.

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:37.757
You heard today, or yesterday,
about the Apache Pass feature

00:29:37.757 --> 00:29:39.590
set that's been put
onto the Intel platform.

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:41.495
And there's many, many
more things coming.

00:29:41.495 --> 00:29:44.120
And what's great about Google is
they're adopting that quickly.

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:46.465
So that's a big tick.

00:29:46.465 --> 00:29:48.090
The second thing,
though, we're hearing

00:29:48.090 --> 00:29:51.510
from customers is, this is
great, but what about security?

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:54.120
Because despite the fact that
Google's whole Cloud platform

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:57.570
was built with security
as part of the DNA,

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:00.430
people don't really know
how to use it properly.

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:02.125
A lot of them have
system administrators

00:30:02.125 --> 00:30:04.250
that haven't really had
the experience of deploying

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:06.240
and scaling Cloud yet.

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:08.994
A lot of them don't know
how to do sandboxing.

00:30:08.994 --> 00:30:11.160
And a lot of them are also
concerned about latencies

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:13.770
because some of them have
very constrained systems

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:17.590
where the infrastructure needs
to be pretty close to the data.

00:30:17.590 --> 00:30:20.100
So that's why we set up ECCoE.

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:22.270
ECCoE is a physical facility.

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:24.000
The first one we've
got is in the UK.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.180
We're planning to put
dots all over the map.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:29.520
And what it's doing is
allowing organizations

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:32.310
to really get a sense of the
power of the Google Cloud

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:34.944
Platform in terms of HPC.

00:30:34.944 --> 00:30:36.360
Now, how we're
doing that is we're

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:39.150
not just providing access
to Google Cloud and people.

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:43.530
What we're doing is real hybrid
performance benchmarking.

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:47.670
What that means is we've got a
facility with an in-house Intel

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:50.220
on-premise platform as
well as the Google Cloud.

00:30:50.220 --> 00:30:53.310
So customers can start seeing
which workloads make sense

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:56.490
to put in the Cloud and
which workloads makes sense

00:30:56.490 --> 00:30:57.600
to leave on-prem.

00:30:57.600 --> 00:31:00.000
Because we're not saying that
Google Cloud right now is

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:01.530
the answer to all the workloads.

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:04.080
There are some workloads,
there are many workloads,

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:05.940
that are very
sensitive that have

00:31:05.940 --> 00:31:07.980
to be run on on-prem systems.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:11.880
But this offers an opportunity
to really start testing

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:14.460
that in different environments.

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:17.190
What we also have is
partnerships with some

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:18.360
of the specialized ISVs.

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.380
So we talked about Altair.

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:25.790
And if you can wander down
to Moscone West to the

00:31:25.790 --> 00:31:27.540
where the Intel stand
is, were actually

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:31.140
showing some of the work we
are doing with Altair and PBS

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:32.160
there.

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:34.080
And how can we start
optimizing orchestration?

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:37.830
How can we start doing it across
several different applications?

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:40.440
What you also get with
the ECCoE environment is

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:43.560
direct access to some of the
key staff in Appsbroker, Google,

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:46.110
and Intel, who are
currently looking at some

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:48.930
of the new feature
sets and really start

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:51.810
enabling sort of a
hybrid cloud environment,

00:31:51.810 --> 00:31:53.730
start doing real sandboxing.

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:56.490
One of the cool things, as well,
is wherever we're learning,

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:58.350
we're turning those into tools.

00:31:58.350 --> 00:32:00.000
So in terms of
orchestration, we're

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:03.630
looking at how we start doing
consistent deployment of VMs

00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:07.811
and containers at scale,
which enables this.

00:32:07.811 --> 00:32:09.810
We're also looking at
tools for cost management,

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:12.018
as well, because one of the
things that customers are

00:32:12.018 --> 00:32:13.950
concerned about when
moving into the cloud

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:15.499
is how do I contain the cost?

00:32:15.499 --> 00:32:17.040
How does this not
get out of control?

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:18.870
It's great that I'm
innovating at speed.

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:20.847
But how do I avoid this
getting out of control?

00:32:20.847 --> 00:32:23.430
And that's the value that we're
providing as part of the ECCoE

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:24.970
environment.

00:32:24.970 --> 00:32:29.510
Now in order to realize scale,
one of the things we recognize

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:31.760
is that you need to have a
proof of concept initially.

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:34.740
And we call our proof
of concept process Three

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:36.970
Steps to Extraordinary.

00:32:36.970 --> 00:32:40.530
Now, what this does is
helps both business leaders

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:42.360
and technical folk
in organizations

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:46.590
get a sense of what the business
case is, get a sense of what

00:32:46.590 --> 00:32:49.200
the benchmarking, what the
performance is, and also

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:52.009
what the roadmap should look
like for your organization.

00:32:52.009 --> 00:32:53.550
So as part of that,
we've established

00:32:53.550 --> 00:32:57.060
what we call the Appsbroker
blueprint, which really helps

00:32:57.060 --> 00:32:59.940
work with you to get a sense
of exactly how your HPC is

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:02.680
performing at the moment.

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:06.120
And from that, moving that
into a workshop environment

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:08.040
within the ECCoE
environment where

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:10.080
we can start doing
some optioneering,

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:13.740
start not just doing whiteboards
in terms of what workload

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:16.020
should go in which place,
what kind of orchestration

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:19.090
is possible, but actually
testing that real time.

00:33:19.090 --> 00:33:21.510
So now you have an opportunity
within a lab environment

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:24.260
to start sandboxing
properly across both on-prem

00:33:24.260 --> 00:33:25.760
and off-prem.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:28.830
Start trying different
types of orchestration.

00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:32.010
And the results of these Three
Steps Extraordinary process

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:33.900
is effectively a
business case which

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:37.170
gives you the cost benefits,
gives you the performance

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:38.970
benefits, which
workloads perform,

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:41.970
which don't, and also
gives you a roadmap.

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:43.900
Now, that's pretty
critical for customers.

00:33:43.900 --> 00:33:46.191
And in fact somebody that
we're working with live right

00:33:46.191 --> 00:33:47.910
now is European hedge fund.

00:33:47.910 --> 00:33:52.830
There is an immense demand in
buy-side financial services

00:33:52.830 --> 00:33:55.410
in terms of quants to start
taking advantage of Google

00:33:55.410 --> 00:33:59.250
Cloud, and a massive demand.

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:01.350
But one of the challenges
that they keep on facing

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:04.420
is, well, how do we
make this secure?

00:34:04.420 --> 00:34:06.570
How do we start actually
using the capacity

00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:09.789
in the right way for
different types of workloads?

00:34:09.789 --> 00:34:11.580
You've got quants that
are doing algorithms

00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:14.400
on significant amounts
of external data,

00:34:14.400 --> 00:34:17.550
significant amounts of
internal data, who currently

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:22.380
are constrained to
develop new algorithms

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.239
or are constrained
because algorithms take

00:34:24.239 --> 00:34:26.280
too long to run on systems.

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:29.880
This means millions of
dollars, millions of dollars,

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:32.150
for these organizations.

00:34:32.150 --> 00:34:35.380
And so what we're
doing is working

00:34:35.380 --> 00:34:37.460
with one of the
larger hedge funds

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:40.949
to look at, firstly, setting
up a hybrid data set up,

00:34:40.949 --> 00:34:43.590
so mimicking what they need in
the future, which is probably

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:46.864
in a bit of an on-premise
as well as a cloud platform.

00:34:46.864 --> 00:34:48.530
Start establishing
provisioning, helping

00:34:48.530 --> 00:34:50.029
them to start
provisioning at scale,

00:34:50.029 --> 00:34:51.440
both on-prem and off-prem.

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:53.780
Helping to do set-ups
and tear-downs

00:34:53.780 --> 00:34:55.080
as quickly as possible.

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:56.830
Also, reproduce a third
party environment.

00:34:56.830 --> 00:34:59.250
Bring the ISVs into the mix.

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:02.420
In the same way we talked
about Altair and crash testing,

00:35:02.420 --> 00:35:04.610
we're going to do the
same in the quant space.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:06.457
Bring everybody
into the environment

00:35:06.457 --> 00:35:08.040
because we don't
know all the answers.

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:09.570
But if we bring
everybody in, we're

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:10.957
going to get the right answers.

00:35:10.957 --> 00:35:13.040
And one of the things that
we're looking, as well,

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:16.400
with this particular fund is
how with the workload manager

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:17.900
you can start
allocating workloads

00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:20.090
between different
types of environment.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:23.300
And what's exciting is that the
moment people start recognizing

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:25.430
the potential is they
start looking at some

00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:29.044
of the other Google products
such as TensorFlow and Cloud AI

00:35:29.044 --> 00:35:30.710
and look at, well,
hey, we can use these

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:32.360
to create whole new models.

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:33.920
This is the exciting
thing about when

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:36.020
people start
culturally recognizing

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:39.641
the art of the possible
through this ECCoE environment.

00:35:39.641 --> 00:35:42.140
And one of the other things
that we've helped the hedge fund

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:44.600
organizations is we showed
them our cost control

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:47.020
dashboard, which really
takes a view of what

00:35:47.020 --> 00:35:51.140
your likely spend might be, and
starts notifying and messaging

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:53.750
people in terms of when
things may run out.

00:35:53.750 --> 00:35:56.790
So it gives you control
over that spend, as well.

00:35:56.790 --> 00:36:01.040
So in summary, I think we're
at an inflection point.

00:36:01.040 --> 00:36:04.280
All this technology that Bill
and Bill spoke about earlier

00:36:04.280 --> 00:36:05.870
is fantastic.

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:07.952
But it's really usable now.

00:36:07.952 --> 00:36:09.660
And I think coming
back to the question I

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:11.990
asked at the beginning
of the presentation

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:14.750
is, why are you waiting?

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:16.684
Why are you waiting to innovate?

00:36:16.684 --> 00:36:19.100
If you're running a design
business, if you need insights,

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:20.570
why are you waiting?

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:22.280
The time is now.

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:23.960
And certainly, if
you can make your way

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:27.110
down to the Intel
stand in Moscone West,

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:28.790
we'd love to talk to you.

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:30.590
We'd love to see how
we could potentially

00:36:30.590 --> 00:36:32.990
do a proof of concept
with your organization

00:36:32.990 --> 00:36:36.020
and start making this real.

00:36:36.020 --> 00:36:37.640
Thank you very much.

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:43.300
